ckness: initial; text-decoration-style: initial; text-decoration-color=
: initial"><SPAN=20
style=3D"FONT-SIZE: 12pt; COLOR: black">This course is to link common =
public=20
health questions with appropriate statistical procedures; to offer a s=
trong=20
conceptual grounding in several important statistical procedures; and =
to=20
describe how to interpret the results from the statistics in ways that=
 are=20
principled and will be persuasive to intended audiences using simple s=
oftware=20
tools. Statistics are a mainstay in the toolkit of public health profe=
ssionals.=20
Human memory being what it is, however, even Public health professiona=
ls with=20
reasonable statistical training, over the years, often forget the basi=
cs. If=20
professionals are going to make sensible use of consultants, communica=
te=20
effectively with funders, and understand others&rsquo; reports, then t=
hey often need=20
at least a conceptual understanding of relatively complex, recently de=
veloped=20
statistical techniques.</SPAN></P>
